<div class="main-title">Likutay Halachos</div>
<div class="sub-title">Choshen Mishpat II</div>

<div class="halacha-title">Laws of Lost and Found (<i>Avaidah u'Metziah</i>) &ndash; 1</div>

<!-- Section 1 -->
<div class="section-num">&sect; 1</div>

<p>Based on what Rabbainu, whose light should shine, wrote in the discourse <span class="verse">&ldquo;And Hashem G&ndash;d built the rib&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="inline-source">(Likutay Moharan I, 67)</span> &mdash; that all the <span class="key-term">neshamos</span> [souls] are clothed within <span class="key-term">kavod</span> [honor/glory], for <span class="key-term">kavod</span> is the &ldquo;mother of all life.&rdquo; And sometimes the <span class="key-term">neshamah</span> [soul] becomes distanced from the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, and then the soul is <span class="emphasis">weary</span>. This occurs through not praying with intention of the heart (<span class="heb-word">kavanas halev</span>), in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;With their lips they honored Me, but their heart was distant&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Isaiah 29:13)</span>. For the intention of the heart is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> [soul], in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;To You, Hashem, I lift my soul&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Psalms 25:1)</span> &mdash; and Rashi explains: &ldquo;I direct my heart.&rdquo; And then one must revive the soul through the aspect of &ldquo;cold waters,&rdquo; and &ldquo;cold waters&rdquo; are produced through the aspect of &ldquo;thunders&rdquo; (<span class="heb-word">re&rsquo;amim</span>). And these come about through giving <span class="key-term">kavod</span> to an elder who has forgotten his learning. See there well, for the distancing of the soul from the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> causes forgetfulness to the elder, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;And the wisdom of his sages shall be lost&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Isaiah 29:14)</span>. See there well.</p>

<!-- Section 2 -->
<div class="section-num">&sect; 2</div>

<p>This is the aspect of the mitzvah of <span class="emphasis">returning a lost object</span> (<span class="key-term">hashavas avaidah</span>). For the lost object (<span class="key-term">avaidah</span>) is the aspect of the distancing of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> from the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>. For all possessions (<span class="heb-word">chafatzim</span>) and wealth are the aspect of <span class="key-term">nefashos</span> [souls], for all the souls have their root in wealth, and their root is in <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, for <span class="key-term">kavod</span> is the root of all the <span class="key-term">nefashos</span>, as mentioned above. Therefore, <i>Rebbi</i> [Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi] would honor the wealthy, and he would give honor to each person according to his wealth &mdash; he would say: &ldquo;Give honor to the one who possesses a hundred <i>maneh</i>; give honor to the one who possesses two hundred <i>maneh</i>&rdquo; &mdash; for each person, according to his wealth, so is his root deeper in the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>. Therefore he would give honor to each one according to his wealth. And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Who is wealthy? One whose townspeople honor him on account of his wealth&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Yerushalmi Shabbos 8)</span>.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;wealth and honor (<i>osher v&rsquo;chavod</i>)&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(I Chronicles 29:12)</span>, for wealth has its root in <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, as mentioned above. And the essence of <span class="key-term">kavod</span> is specifically with the human being, as is brought in the words of Rabbainu, whose light should shine &mdash; that the essence of <span class="key-term">kavod</span> is only within the domain of the human being, who is the <i>speaker</i> (<span class="heb-word">m&rsquo;daber</span>). See there. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;With their lips they honored Me&rdquo;</span>. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;And in His sanctuary, all of it declares: Honor!&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Psalms 29:9)</span> &mdash; &ldquo;<i>haichal</i>&rdquo; [sanctuary] refers to the mouth, as is known.</p>

<p>Therefore, when something becomes lost from a person, it is the aspect of the distancing of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> that is within the lost object from the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, since it is far from the person, and it is with the person that the essence of <span class="key-term">kavod</span> resides. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;perishing without a garment&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Job 24:7)</span>, for every aspect of <span class="key-term">avaidah</span> [loss] is &ldquo;without a garment&rdquo; &mdash; namely, the aspect of distancing from the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, which is a &ldquo;garment&rdquo; (<span class="heb-word">levush</span>), for the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> is clothed in the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-word">ovaid</span> [&ldquo;perishing&rdquo;], for the matter is stated regarding the lost object itself &mdash; namely, that it goes to perdition. As Rashi explained on the verse <span class="verse">&ldquo;I was like a lost vessel&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Psalms 31:13)</span> &mdash; that every expression of <span class="key-term">avaidah</span> [loss], etc. &mdash; for the object goes to perdition since it is distant from the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, and then it is in the aspect of a <span class="emphasis">weary soul</span> that goes to perdition.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;When you encounter your brother&rsquo;s donkey going astray&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Exodus 23:4)</span>, for surely the lost object is in the aspect of &ldquo;going astray&rdquo; (<span class="heb-word">so&rsquo;eh</span>) and &ldquo;wandering,&rdquo; since it is distanced from its &ldquo;mother&rdquo; &mdash; namely, from the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> &mdash; in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;And it was, when G&ndash;d caused me to wander from my father&rsquo;s house&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Genesis 20:13)</span>, as Rashi explained there. Therefore, it is a great mitzvah to return a lost object, and thereby one returns the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> to the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, as mentioned above.</p>

<!-- Section 3 -->
<div class="section-num">&sect; 3</div>

<p>Therefore, the banner of the camp of Dan was the <span class="emphasis">gatherer for all the camps</span> (<span class="heb-word">m&rsquo;asaif l&rsquo;chol hamachanos</span>), and Rashi explained that they would return all the lost items of Israel. For the camp of Dan is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> [Kingship], which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">tefillah</span> [prayer], as is brought in the words of Rabbainu, whose light should shine, in the discourse <span class="verse">&ldquo;And on the day of the first-fruits&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Likutay Moharan I, 56)</span>. See there well &mdash; that the camp of Dan is the aspect of <span class="heb-word">dina d&rsquo;malchusa</span> [the law of the kingdom]. See there. Therefore it is the aspect of <span class="key-term">tefillah</span>, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;Judge me, O G&ndash;d, and also hear my voice&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Psalms 43:1; Genesis 30:6)</span>, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of &ldquo;gatherer for all the camps&rdquo; &mdash; that they would gather all the camps of holiness. That is, the aspect of <span class="key-term">tefillah</span> with <span class="key-term">kavanah</span> [intention], through which one gathers and collects all the camps into the holiness, in the aspect of returning the <span class="key-term">nefashos</span> to their mother and root &mdash; namely, to the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>. For this is the essence of <span class="key-term">tefillah</span> with <span class="key-term">kavanah</span>, as mentioned above &mdash; namely, to bind and gather all the thoughts and desires and souls into the words of the <span class="key-term">tefillah</span>; to collect the scattering of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> &mdash; this being the aspect of the thoughts that scatter during <span class="key-term">tefillah</span> to the outside &mdash; and one must gather the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> from the scattering, so that he has no extraneous thought, but rather binds the thought to the speech with a strong and firm bond. And then he is a &ldquo;gatherer for all the camps,&rdquo; as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of <span class="emphasis">returning the lost object</span>, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>Therefore, the children of Dan, who were gatherers for all the camps, would return all the lost items, as Rashi explained &mdash; that for this reason they were called &ldquo;gatherer,&rdquo; etc., as mentioned above. And therefore <span class="emphasis">Amalaik</span>, who stands opposed to the holy <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> and constantly pursues the prayer of Israel &mdash; therefore they would pursue the children of Dan, as Rabbainu, whose light should shine, wrote there. Therefore it says regarding him: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And he struck at your rear, all the stragglers behind you, and you were weary and exhausted&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Deuteronomy 25:18)</span>. <span class="precise">&ldquo;Weary and exhausted&rdquo; precisely</span> &mdash; the aspect of weariness of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span>, through having become distanced from the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, through the blemish of <span class="key-term">tefillah</span> without intention of the heart, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>And this is <span class="verse">&ldquo;and he did not fear G&ndash;d&rdquo;</span> &mdash; namely, the blemish of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> and <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, for the essence of <span class="key-term">kavod</span> is <span class="key-term">yirah</span> [awe] &mdash; to be in awe before His glory, may He be blessed, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;to fear the honored, glorious Name&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Deuteronomy 28:58)</span>.</p>

<p>Therefore Amalaik is the aspect of <span class="emphasis">forgetfulness</span> (<span class="heb-word">shich&rsquo;chah</span>), as is known, for he causes the aspect of &ldquo;an elder who has forgotten his learning,&rdquo; through the weariness of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span>, through the blemish of <span class="key-term">tefillah</span>, as mentioned above. Therefore there is a mitzvah to <span class="emphasis">remember</span> the deeds of Amalaik, in order to rectify the aspect of forgetfulness mentioned above.</p>

<p>And this is what is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And Amalaik came and fought with Israel in Refidim&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Exodus 17:8)</span>. And our Sages of blessed memory said: &ldquo;[<i>biR&rsquo;fidim</i>] &mdash; in laxity of hands (<span class="heb-word">rifyon yadayim</span>)&rdquo; &mdash; this is the aspect of laxity and weariness of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span>. For the growth and strengthening of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> comes through the aspect of the <span class="emphasis">hands</span>, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;and in Your hand to make great and to strengthen&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(I Chronicles 29:12)</span>, as explained there in the discourse <span class="verse">&ldquo;And [Hashem] built&rdquo;</span> mentioned above. See there. And so, when there is laxity of hands, G&ndash;d forbid, it is the aspect of weariness of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span>, and then &ldquo;Amalaik came,&rdquo; as mentioned above.</p>

<p>And this is what is written first: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And they encamped at Refidim, and there was no water to drink&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Exodus 17:1)</span>. For there, in the laxity of hands, which is the aspect of weariness of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span>, they needed water &mdash; the aspect of &ldquo;cold waters&rdquo; mentioned above. But they blemished further: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And the people quarreled with Moshe&rdquo;</span> &mdash; and they blemished the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> of the elder. Therefore Moshe cried out to Hashem: <span class="verse">&ldquo;What shall I do with this people? A little more and they will stone me!&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Exodus 17:4)</span> &mdash; for &ldquo;I do not know what to do for them, for the matter depends on me &mdash; through their giving me <span class="key-term">kavod</span>,&rdquo; as mentioned above, and they are blemishing this further, and through this they cause the aspect of forgetfulness to the elder.</p>

<p>And this is <span class="precise">&ldquo;a little more and they will stone me&rdquo;</span> &mdash; this is the aspect of forgetfulness, which is the aspect of death, as Rabbainu, whose light should shine, wrote <span class="inline-source">(Likutay Moharan I, 37)</span>. And this is &ldquo;<i>m&rsquo;at</i>&rdquo; [a little] &mdash; it is the aspect of forgetfulness, which is <span class="heb-word">mochin d&rsquo;katnus</span> [constricted consciousness], as is known.</p>

<p>Therefore Hashem, may He be blessed, answered him: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Pass before the people&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Exodus 17:5)</span> &mdash; and you will see that they will conduct themselves with <span class="key-term">kavod</span> toward you, in the manner that Rashi explained there. And then it was indeed rectified. And he took the staff, which is the aspect of a ruling scepter, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, as our Sages of blessed memory said: &ldquo;Take a staff and strike upon their skulls; one spokesman for the generation,&rdquo; etc. And through this he struck the rock, which is the aspect of a stone upon the mouth of the well &mdash; which is the aspect of clouds that cover the eyes, through which it is impossible to draw from the waters of <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> [knowledge], the aspect of &ldquo;cold waters&rdquo; mentioned above. And through striking the rock with the staff, as mentioned above &mdash; which is the aspect of &ldquo;thunders&rdquo; &mdash; through this, great waters came forth, the aspect of &ldquo;cold waters,&rdquo; <span class="verse">&ldquo;and the congregation and their livestock drank&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Numbers 20:11)</span>. And all the <span class="key-term">nefashos</span> were rectified and revived from their weariness. See well in the discourse <span class="verse">&ldquo;And [Hashem] built&rdquo;</span> mentioned above, regarding the aspects of &ldquo;thunders&rdquo; and &ldquo;cold waters.&rdquo; See there.</p>

<p>Therefore the passage of Amalaik is juxtaposed [to this], for through that very blemish which was not yet fully rectified, as it is written <span class="verse">&ldquo;because of their testing,&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="inline-source">(Exodus 17:7)</span> &mdash; therefore &ldquo;Amalaik came,&rdquo; as mentioned above. And therefore the war with Amalaik was dependent on <span class="key-term">tefillah</span> with <span class="key-term">kavanah</span>, as it is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And it was, when Moshe raised his hand, Israel prevailed&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Exodus 17:11)</span>. And our Sages of blessed memory said: &ldquo;Were the hands of Moshe [winning or losing the war]? Rather, when Israel would gaze upward and direct their hearts to their Father in Heaven, they would prevail,&rdquo; etc. <span class="inline-source">(Rosh HaShanah 29a)</span>. For the essence of the victory over Amalaik was dependent on the intention of the heart in <span class="key-term">tefillah</span>, to rectify the weariness of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span>, as mentioned above. And all of this was dependent on Moshe, for from him is drawn the aspect of &ldquo;cold waters,&rdquo; as mentioned above.</p>

<!-- Section 4 -->
<div class="section-num">&sect; 4</div>

<p>It emerges that through <span class="emphasis">returning a lost object</span>, the aspect of holy <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, is rectified. For just as the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> is weary when it falls and becomes lost and distanced from its &ldquo;mother&rdquo; &mdash; namely, the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> &mdash; so too is the power of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> and <span class="key-term">kavod</span> blemished and weakened when the <span class="key-term">nefashos</span> are distant from it, for <span class="verse">&ldquo;there is no king without a people&rdquo;</span>.</p>

<p>Therefore, the kingship of Israel began initially through <span class="emphasis">returning a lost object</span> &mdash; that Shaul went to seek his father&rsquo;s lost [donkeys]. And this is what is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And as for the donkeys that were lost&hellip; do not set your heart upon them, for they have been found. And to whom does all the desire of Israel belong?&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="inline-source">(I Samuel 9:20)</span>. For this depends on that, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>And all of this came about because they blemished through their request, saying: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Give us a king to judge us, like all the nations&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(I Samuel 8:5)</span>. It turns out that they distanced themselves from the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, for the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> belongs exclusively to Israel, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;And in their assembly, let my honor not be joined&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Genesis 49:6)</span>, as Rabbainu, whose light should shine, wrote. But the wicked kingdom, which is the aspect of Aisav-Amalaik, is in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;despised are you exceedingly&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Obadiah 1:2)</span>. And they said, &ldquo;Give us a king like all the nations,&rdquo; and they distanced themselves from the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> and blemished the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> of the elder &mdash; namely, Shmuel &mdash; as it is written at the beginning of the passage: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And it was, when Shmuel grew old&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(I Samuel 8:1)</span>. For they needed to be careful with his <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, through which the kingship of Israel would be rectified, as mentioned above. And as is apparent there in the verses &mdash; that they blemished this greatly, for they were not careful with the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> of Shmuel. Therefore, Hashem arranged the matter so that there would be a lost item near the place of the kingship &mdash; namely, in the house of Kish, father of Shaul. And afterwards, when the lost item was found, then the kingship was rectified, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;And as for the lost donkeys&hellip; and to whom does all the desire of Israel belong? Is it not to you and to all your father&rsquo;s house?&rdquo;</span> as mentioned above <span class="inline-source">(I Samuel 9, 10, 13)</span>.</p>

<p>Therefore he was warned: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Seven days shall you wait until I come to you&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(I Samuel 10:8)</span>. For his kingship depended on this &mdash; to be very careful with the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> of Shmuel, as mentioned above, in order to rectify the blemish mentioned above. And therefore, when he was not careful with this, his kingship was annulled, as it is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And now your kingdom shall not endure&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="inline-source">(I Samuel 13:14)</span>. For the rectification of the kingship depended on the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> of Shmuel, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>Therefore, the passage of Amalaik was immediately juxtaposed, and he was commanded to destroy Amalaik. For then he needed to gird his loins to fight Amalaik, since they had not been careful with the aspect of rectifying the return of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> to the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, as mentioned above, and then comes the war of Amalaik, as mentioned above. And this is what Rashi explained there: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And now, hear the voice of the word of Hashem&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(I Samuel 15:1)</span> &mdash; &ldquo;once you acted foolishly; now hear,&rdquo; etc. This is connected to this, for the war of Amalaik comes through the blemish mentioned above &mdash; that he blemished the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> of the elder, as mentioned above. And therefore he needed to be careful now to erase the memory of Amalaik, lest he grow strong, G&ndash;d forbid. And since he was not careful even with this, his kingship was torn away completely, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: that Shaul was punished for not insisting on his <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, as it is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And they despised him, and he was as one who was silent&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(I Samuel 10:27)</span>. For he certainly needed to insist on his <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, for the rectification of the kingship is through the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, as mentioned above. And therefore Shmuel said to him: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Is it not so that even if you are small in your own eyes, you are the head of the tribes of Israel?&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(I Samuel 15:17)</span> &mdash; and you needed to be careful with your <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, for that is the essence of the rectification of the kingship, as mentioned above. And because he did not insist on his <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, through this he caused the war of Amalaik, as mentioned above, and he needed to be very careful now to subdue him. And because he blemished even in this, his kingship was torn away.</p>

<p>It emerges that all these blemishes were connected to each other, and because he was not careful with them, his kingship was discontinued. Therefore [Shmuel] tore his garment &mdash; to indicate that the entire essence of the blemish is the aspect of blemishing the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, which is the aspect of a &ldquo;garment&rdquo; (<span class="heb-word">levush</span>), as mentioned above. As they said <span class="inline-source">(Bava Kama 91b)</span>: Rabbi Yochanan called his garments &ldquo;my honorers&rdquo; (<span class="heb-word">m&rsquo;chab&rsquo;dusai</span>). And through this they caused that Shmuel said <span class="verse">&ldquo;indeed, before Hashem&rsquo;s anointed&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(I Samuel 16:6)</span>, and Hashem, may He be blessed, responded to him: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Do not look at his appearance&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(I Samuel 16:7)</span> &mdash; which is the aspect of a blemish in the <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> of Shmuel the prophet, the aspect of &ldquo;an elder who has forgotten his learning,&rdquo; in proportion to the greatness of his stature.</p>

<!-- Section 5 -->
<div class="section-num">&sect; 5</div>

<p>And this is when Shmuel rebuked them for having blemished his <span class="key-term">kavod</span> and having asked for a king, he said: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Is it not&hellip; and he called to Hashem, and He sent thunder and rain&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(I Samuel 12:17&ndash;18)</span> &mdash; namely, he told them that even now he has the power to draw down the aspect of &ldquo;thunders&rdquo; and &ldquo;cold waters&rdquo; &mdash; and this is &ldquo;thunder and rain.&rdquo; And this is what Rashi explained: &ldquo;And you should not have blemished my <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, since I have this power&rdquo; &mdash; to draw down the aspect of &ldquo;thunders&rdquo; and &ldquo;cold waters,&rdquo; as mentioned above.</p>

<p>And this is what is stated: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And they drew water and poured it out before Hashem&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(I Samuel 7:6)</span>. And Rashi and the Targum explain there that they poured out their hearts like water &mdash; namely, <span class="key-term">tefillah</span> with <span class="key-term">kavanah</span>. And this is the aspect of the verse: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Pour out your heart like water before the face of Hashem&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Lamentations 2:19)</span> &mdash; which speaks of <span class="key-term">tefillah</span> with <span class="key-term">kavanah</span>; namely, that the prayer should be with intention, in order to revive and restore the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> through this. And this is &ldquo;like water&rdquo; &mdash; the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;cold waters upon a weary soul&rdquo;</span> mentioned above <span class="inline-source">(Proverbs 25:25)</span>.</p>

<!-- Section 6 -->
<div class="section-num">&sect; 6</div>

<p>Therefore, the shepherds and kings of Israel were first shepherds and keepers of flocks &mdash; like Yaakov and David and Moshe Rabbainu, peace be upon him. For through guarding the flock so that they would not be lost &mdash; which is the aspect of guarding the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> so that it does not become distanced from the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> &mdash; through this they merited kingship, for this is the aspect of the rectification of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>Therefore the prophet rebukes the shepherds in this very language of the blemish of loss &mdash; that they are not guarding them &mdash; as he said <span class="inline-source">(Ezekiel 34)</span> regarding the shepherds: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Lost sheep were My people&hellip; the lost ones you did not seek&rdquo;</span>. For the essence of the rectification of the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> is through the aspect of returning the lost object &mdash; namely, returning the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> to the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>. For the king must guard his people, who are the <span class="key-term">nefashos</span>, so that they do not scatter and become distanced to the outside and become lost, G&ndash;d forbid.</p>

<p>And this is what is written regarding David <span class="inline-source">(Psalms 78:71&ndash;72)</span>: <span class="verse">&ldquo;From behind the nursing ewes He brought him, to shepherd Yaakov His people.&rdquo;</span> For through guarding the flock exceedingly with a compassionate eye, so that they would not be lost, G&ndash;d forbid, through this he merited to be king, to shepherd His people Israel. For this is the rectification of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, as mentioned above, in the aspect of returning the <span class="key-term">nefashos</span> to the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>And this is: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And he shepherded them with the integrity of his heart&rdquo;</span> &mdash; this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">tefillah</span> with <span class="key-term">kavanah</span>, where his mouth and heart are equal. And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-word">tam laivav</span> [integrity of heart] &mdash; that his heart is not divided from his mouth. And through the aspect of &ldquo;integrity of his heart,&rdquo; the aspect of <span class="key-term">tefillah</span> with <span class="key-term">kavanah</span>, the aspect of the drawing near of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> to the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> &mdash; through this comes the rectification of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, as mentioned above. And this is: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And he shepherded them with the integrity of his heart&rdquo;</span> &mdash; according to the integrity of his heart, so was he a shepherd and king, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;and with the skill of his hands he guided them&rdquo;</span> &mdash; these are the aspect of the <span class="emphasis">hands</span> which make the <span class="key-term">nefashos</span> grow and become strong, as mentioned above.</p>

<!-- Section 7 -->
<div class="section-num">&sect; 7</div>

<p>And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="verse">&ldquo;If he was an elder and it is not befitting his dignity (<span class="key-term">kavod</span>)&rdquo;</span> &mdash; regarding this it says: <span class="verse">&ldquo;and you may hide yourself&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Deuteronomy 22:1)</span>. For the lost object is the aspect of the distancing of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> from the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, as mentioned above, and then it is in the aspect of a &ldquo;weary soul,&rdquo; as mentioned above. And the essential rectification for this depends on the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> of the elder, as mentioned above. For when there is a lost object, a certain blemish reaches the <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> of the elder, the aspect of &ldquo;an elder who has forgotten his learning,&rdquo; as mentioned above &mdash; for this depends on that, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>Therefore, the Torah commanded <span class="verse">&ldquo;and you may hide yourself&rdquo;</span> &mdash; so that the elder does not blemish his <span class="key-term">kavod</span> through the lost object. For on the contrary, now one must be very careful with the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> of the elder &mdash; and this itself is the aspect of the rectification of the lost object, as mentioned above.</p>

<!-- Section 8 -->
<div class="section-num">&sect; 8</div>

<p>And this is what is written in the <i>Shulchan Aruch</i> [Code of Jewish Law]: &ldquo;A lost object of a gentile is permitted [to keep], and it is forbidden to return it,&rdquo; etc. &ldquo;And if he returned it for the sake of <span class="key-term">kiddush Hashem</span> [sanctification of G&ndash;d&rsquo;s Name], it is permitted. And in a place where there would be <span class="key-term">chillul Hashem</span> [desecration of G&ndash;d&rsquo;s Name], he is obligated to return it,&rdquo; etc.</p>

<p>For the gentile nations have no portion in the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>; for the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> belongs only to Israel, but they are in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;despised are you exceedingly,&rdquo;</span> as mentioned above. Therefore, their lost object is certainly permitted, for the essence of returning a lost object is to return the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> to the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, as mentioned above. And on the contrary, it is <span class="emphasis">forbidden</span> to return it, for the <span class="key-term">nefashos</span> that are in the hands of the nations are like birds caught in a trap, and since they have escaped from the fowler&rsquo;s snare, it is a great mitzvah that a Jew should acquire them &mdash; for there is the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> &mdash; in order to revive them, to return them to the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>.</p>

<p>Therefore, in a place where there is <span class="key-term">kiddush Hashem</span>, where the Name of Heaven will be sanctified and honored through returning the lost object to the gentile, then it is permitted to return it. For through this one causes <span class="key-term">kavod shamayim</span> [honor of Heaven]. Therefore, even though he returns it to the gentile, it is nevertheless in the aspect of returning the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> to the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, since G&ndash;d&rsquo;s honor, may He be blessed, is sanctified and magnified through this, as mentioned above. And similarly, in a place where there would be <span class="key-term">chillul Hashem</span>, he is obligated to return it, so as not to blemish <span class="key-term">kavod shamayim</span>, G&ndash;d forbid, through the lost object. For the essence of the rectification of the lost object is through the aspect of rectifying the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, as mentioned above.</p>

<!-- Section 9 [missing in text - skipped from 8 to 10] -->

<!-- Section 10 -->
<div class="section-num">&sect; 10</div>

<p>And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="verse">&ldquo;If his father told him, &lsquo;Do not return the lost object,&rsquo; he should not listen to him&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Bava Metzia 32a)</span>. For returning the lost object is the aspect of rectifying the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, as mentioned above. And therefore, one might have thought (<span class="heb-word">havah amina</span>) that honoring one&rsquo;s father should override this mitzvah, for honoring one&rsquo;s father is also the aspect of rectifying the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> &mdash; for the root of one&rsquo;s soul is in one&rsquo;s father and mother, where one&rsquo;s soul is clothed, and the primary clothing of the soul is in the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, as mentioned above. Therefore he is obligated to honor them, for they are his <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, for there his soul is clothed.</p>

<p>And therefore the Torah teaches us that even so, their honor does not override the mitzvah of returning a lost object, which is also the aspect of rectifying the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, as mentioned above. And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: &ldquo;His father told him, &lsquo;Do not return&rsquo; &mdash; one might think he should listen to him? The verse teaches: &lsquo;I am Hashem&rsquo; &mdash; <span class="precise">all of you are obligated in My honor</span>.&rdquo; <span class="precise">&ldquo;In My honor&rdquo; precisely</span>, for this is the aspect of rectifying the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, as mentioned above.</p>

<!-- Section 11 -->
<div class="section-num">&sect; 11</div>

<p>And this is why our Sages of blessed memory established and placed the laws of the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> of one&rsquo;s teacher and the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> of one&rsquo;s father specifically in connection with a lost object, as they said in the Mishnah <span class="inline-source">(Bava Metzia 33a)</span>: &ldquo;His father&rsquo;s lost object and his teacher&rsquo;s lost object,&rdquo; etc. For a son or student must pursue the lost object of his father and teacher all the more, since he is obligated in their <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, and returning a lost object is also the rectification of the <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>Therefore Shaul went to seek his father&rsquo;s lost [donkeys], for the son must pursue his father&rsquo;s lost object, and then the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> is completed and greatly rectified, as mentioned above. And likewise the student for his teacher, as mentioned above. And therefore they established the laws of their <span class="key-term">kavod</span> <span class="precise">specifically</span> in connection with a lost object, as mentioned above.</p>

<!-- Section 12 -->
<div class="section-num">&sect; 12</div>

<p>Therefore, the found object (<span class="key-term">metziah</span>) of one&rsquo;s son and daughter belongs to the father. And as long as they eat at his table, they are considered minors (<span class="heb-word">k&rsquo;tanim</span>), and their found objects belong to their father. For when the finder acquires the found object for himself, he returns the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> to the portion of <span class="key-term">kavod</span> that is his own and he acquires it. Therefore, minors &mdash; as long as they eat at their father&rsquo;s table, their found objects belong to him. For the table is the aspect of <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, the aspect of &ldquo;a king&rsquo;s table,&rdquo; as is explained there. And therefore, as long as they eat from his table, they are attached to his <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, and they must return their found objects to him, for there is their <span class="key-term">kavod</span>, since they eat from his.</p>

<!-- Section 13 -->
<div class="section-num">&sect; 13</div>

<p>Also, all acquisitions (<span class="heb-word">z&rsquo;chiyos</span>) are through <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> [knowledge/mind]. Therefore a minor has no capacity for acquisition, for he has no <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span>. And likewise a deaf-mute and one who lacks mental competence (<span class="heb-word">shoteh</span>). For wealth is the root of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span>, and its root is in <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span>, for <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> is the root of all sustenance, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;and through knowledge the chambers are filled&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Proverbs 24:4)</span>. For wealth and the <span class="key-term">nefashos</span> are the aspect of <span class="heb-word">g&rsquo;vuros</span> [strengths/severities], as Rabbainu, whose light should shine, wrote &mdash; that their root is in <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span>. And similarly it is implied in his words many times that wealth depends on <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span>. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;In the shadow of wisdom, in the shadow of money&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Ecclesiastes 7:12)</span>.</p>

<p>Therefore, whoever has no <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> has no capacity for acquisition in any form of wealth &mdash; that is, in any object. And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">ya&rsquo;ush</span> [despair/renunciation] that effects acquisition in a found object. For <span class="key-term">ya&rsquo;ush</span> is when one diverts his mind (<span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span>) from the matter, as they said: &ldquo;Despair that is not conscious (<span class="heb-word">ya&rsquo;ush shelo mida&rsquo;as</span>) is not [valid] despair&rdquo; <span class="inline-source">(Bava Metzia 21b)</span>. For he must know that he lost [the object] and consciously despair of it. It turns out that he removes his <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> from that object which he lost, and therefore he no longer has any acquisition in it, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>Therefore, they are considered minors regarding found objects as long as they eat at their father&rsquo;s table, and their found objects belong to their father. For the essence of <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> comes through eating, as Rabbainu wrote in the discourse <span class="verse">&ldquo;And it was, as they were emptying their sacks&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Likutay Moharan I, 17)</span>. Therefore, as long as they eat at his table, their <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> is attached to their father. Therefore their found objects belong to him, and he acquires the found objects of his children, for the essence of acquisition is through <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span>, as mentioned above.</p>

<!-- Section 14 -->
<div class="section-num">&sect; 14</div>

<p>Therefore, an animal that causes damage (<span class="heb-word">davar hamazik</span>) does not need to be returned to its owner; rather, the one who finds it kills it and acquires its hide, as is written in the <i>Shulchan Aruch</i>. For all damages are the opposite of <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span>, for <span class="verse">&ldquo;anger rests in the bosom of fools&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Ecclesiastes 7:9)</span>, as Rabbainu, whose light should shine, wrote in many places &mdash; that anger and hatred are foolishness. And in the future, when the <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> will be revealed, there will be nothing that causes damage, as it is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And the wolf shall dwell with the lamb&hellip; they shall neither harm nor destroy, for the earth shall be full of the knowledge of Hashem&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="inline-source">(Isaiah 11:6,9)</span>.</p>

<p>Therefore, an animal that causes damage has no acquisition [rights], since it has no <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span>. Therefore it is ownerless (<span class="heb-word">hefkair</span>) to the finder, and the finder kills it and acquires its hide. For after he kills it and eliminates the damage, then there is acquisition in what remains &mdash; namely, in the hide &mdash; since it no longer contains damage, it now has an attachment to <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> like other objects, and there is acquisition in it. Therefore the finder who killed it acquires it, for he brought the matter to the aspect of <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> and acquisition. For initially, when it was causing damage, there was no <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> or acquisition in it, as mentioned above. And therefore he acquires it, through having killed it and brought it to the aspect of <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> and acquisition, as mentioned above.</p>

<!-- Translator Summary -->
<div class="translator-summary">
  <h3>&#9670; Translator&rsquo;s Summary &#9670;</h3>

  <p>This halachah expounds on the laws of lost and found objects through the lens of Rabbainu&rsquo;s teaching in Likutay Moharan I:67 (<i>&ldquo;And Hashem built the rib&rdquo;</i>). The central concept is that all souls (<i>nefashos</i>) are &ldquo;clothed&rdquo; in <i>kavod</i> (honor/glory), which is their &ldquo;mother.&rdquo; When a soul becomes distanced from the <i>kavod</i>, it becomes weary &mdash; and this is the spiritual root of all loss.</p>

  <p>The key connections established are:</p>

  <div class="flowchart">
    <span class="step"><b>Loss / <i>Avaidah</i></b></span>
    <span class="arrow">&darr;</span>
    <span class="step">The <i>nefesh</i> becomes distanced from <i>kavod</i> (glory/honor)</span>
    <span class="arrow">&darr;</span>
    <span class="step">The soul becomes <b>weary</b> &mdash; causes <b>forgetfulness</b> to the elder</span>
    <span class="arrow">&darr;</span>
    <span class="step"><b>Amalaik</b> attacks &mdash; the force of forgetfulness and spiritual desolation</span>
    <span class="arrow">&uarr; <i>Rectification:</i></span>
    <span class="step"><b><i>Tefillah</i> with <i>kavanah</i></b> (prayer with heartfelt intention)</span>
    <span class="arrow">&darr;</span>
    <span class="step">Returns the <i>nefesh</i> to the <i>kavod</i> &rarr; <b>Hashavas Avaidah</b> (returning the lost)</span>
    <span class="arrow">&darr;</span>
    <span class="step">Rectifies <b><i>Malchus</i></b> (Kingship) of holiness</span>
  </div>

  <p>Practical halachic applications are illuminated: why the elder may hide from a lost object beneath his dignity (to protect the <i>kavod</i>); why a gentile&rsquo;s lost object is treated differently (they have no share in the <i>kavod</i> unless <i>kiddush Hashem</i> is involved); why a father&rsquo;s honor does not override the mitzvah of returning a lost object; why a son&rsquo;s or student&rsquo;s found object goes to the father or teacher; and why one who lacks <i>da&rsquo;as</i> has no capacity for acquisition.</p>

  <p>The overarching message: every lost object in this world is a reflection of the great spiritual loss &mdash; the distancing of the <i>nefesh</i> from its root in <i>kavod</i>. The rectification is prayer with the full engagement of the heart, which gathers all scattered souls back to their holy source. And this is the essential task of the true <i>tzadikim</i> and of the <i>Malchus</i> of Israel.</p>
</div>

